== 5.7 Chapter 5 Practice Drill — 40 Questions

40 questions graded in the order the CBT engine is likely to meet them. Allow 44 minutes; the key and a full solution for every question follow.

#mcq(1, [The narrative voice of the novel is],
  [first person by Bepo],
  [third person, limited to Bepo],
  [second person],
  [third person omniscient]
)

#mcq(2, [The limit of the narration means that the reader cannot know],
  [what Bepo remembers],
  [what Bepo fears],
  [what Bepo earns],
  [what Seri thinks when she is alone]
)

#mcq(3, [The title of Chapter 1, *Dusk*, suggests],
  [the end of the school term],
  [Bepo's retirement],
  [the time of the assembly],
  [failing light and approaching darkness]
)

#mcq(4, [The title of Chapter 12, *…Dawn*, suggests],
  [the journey to London],
  [renewal and a new day],
  [another night],
  [the end of the school year]
)

#mcq(5, [The titles of the first and last chapters together form],
  [an aside],
  [a flashback],
  [a frame],
  [a simile]
)

#mcq(6, [Chief Mrs Solape Bayo's remark about the snake in the roof is an example of],
  [a simile about the school building],
  [a proverb used as a metaphor for a danger the board has housed itself],
  [an idiom meaning the school is unsafe],
  [hyperbole used to frighten the teachers]
)

#mcq(7, [The device in *it is like hanging a snake in the roof* is specifically],
  [onomatopoeia],
  [alliteration],
  [personification],
  [a simile]
)

#mcq(8, [The effect of the snake proverb in its scene is to],
  [introduce a new character],
  [compress the board's fear into a single image],
  [show that the chairman is uneducated],
  [delay the board's decision]
)

#mcq(9, [Bepo's shout of *Noooo!* during the drama club's dance is significant because it],
  [shows that he dislikes the performance],
  [proves that he has lost his voice],
  [lets the memory of Badagry break into the present],
  [is the moment he decides to travel]
)

#mcq(10, [The repetition of *Noooo!* in Chapters 11 and 12 is best described as],
  [an aside],
  [a rhyme],
  [a motif],
  [a simile]
)

#mcq(11, [The dream at the airport functions in the novel as],
  [the last turn of the argument inside Bepo],
  [comic relief],
  [a description of London],
  [a digression]
)

#mcq(12, [The Yoruba saying about the carver means that],
  [the work remains after the worker has gone],
  [a craftsman is never appreciated at home],
  [wood lasts longer than stone],
  [the school needs more artisans]
)

#mcq(13, [The novel's use of the word *japa* shows],
  [a technical term from migration studies],
  [a foreign loan word used for decoration],
  [a spelling error in the Nigerian edition],
  [a local idiom that names the theme in the language of the characters]
)

#mcq(14, [The other Nigerian words in the prose — *iru*, *egusi*, *owambe* — chiefly],
  [show that Bepo is uneducated],
  [locate the story in its setting],
  [confuse the reader],
  [date the novel to the 1970s]
)

#mcq(15, [The novel's language is best described as],
  [plain and accessible],
  [poetic throughout],
  [ornate and difficult],
  [archaic]
)

#mcq(16, [Mr Audu's jokes are used in the novel to],
  [replace the narrator],
  [waste space],
  [show that he is foolish],
  [relieve tension while telling the truth]
)

#mcq(17, [The novel's central irony is that],
  [the board is richer than the school],
  [the police arrest the wrong man],
  [the pupils know more than the teachers],
  [a headmaster condemns emigration while preparing to emigrate]
)

#mcq(18, [The tone of the final chapter is best described as],
  [hopeful],
  [comic],
  [indifferent],
  [bitter]
)

#mcq(19, [A flashback is used in Chapter 7 in order to],
  [explain why Bepo left Beesway],
  [fill space],
  [describe Lagos traffic],
  [introduce Seri]
)

#mcq(20, [A flashback in Chapter 2 establishes],
  [Bepo's twenty-four years at Stardom],
  [the Banky–Tosh feud],
  [the history of the school's board],
  [the passport queue]
)

#mcq(21, [A replay of past events inside a later chapter is called],
  [apostrophe],
  [foreshadowing],
  [a flashback],
  [an aside]
)

#mcq(22, [The planting of a detail that prepares the reader for a later event is called],
  [a flashback],
  [a simile],
  [foreshadowing],
  [an ellipsis]
)

#mcq(23, [The chapter whose title is itself a sentence is],
  [Chapter 7],
  [Chapter 6],
  [Chapter 5],
  [Chapter 10]
)

#mcq(24, [The chapter title that names a place in Badagry is],
  [Laughing Waterfalls],
  [Passport Pains],
  [Snake in the Roof],
  [Point of No Return]
)

#mcq(25, [The Point of No Return functions in the novel as],
  [a symbol of a departure that cannot be undone],
  [the name of the airport],
  [a chapter about the passport],
  [a tourist attraction only]
)

#mcq(26, [The *dusk* and *dawn* of the chapter titles are],
  [times of day in the plot],
  [names of schools],
  [characters' surnames],
  [moods that frame the story]
)

#mcq(27, [The narrative returns repeatedly to the same few objects. This technique is called],
  [motif],
  [hyperbole],
  [alliteration],
  [dialogue]
)

#mcq(28, [The narrator's judgements feel like facts because],
  [the novel is written in the first person],
  [the novel has no narrator],
  [the narrator is omniscient],
  [the narration is limited and close to Bepo]
)

#mcq(29, [The reader never visits London because],
  [Seri refuses to be described],
  [the novel is set entirely in Lagos],
  [the author had never been there],
  [the book is about what Nigeria is worth to the man leaving it]
)

#mcq(30, [The novel's humour is important because it],
  [proves the book is a comedy],
  [replaces the plot],
  [lets the book say hard things without preaching],
  [shows that Bepo is happy]
)

#mcq(31, [Which of the following is a chapter title that names an activity rather than a state?],
  [Dusk],
  [The Enticement],
  […Dawn],
  [Laughing Waterfalls]
)

#mcq(32, [The novel's structure — present, memory, present — is best described as],
  [epistolary],
  [episodic with flashbacks],
  [a diary],
  [linear]
)

#mcq(33, [Bepo's repeated *other things being equal* suggests],
  [that he is quoting the MD],
  [that he is unsure of English],
  [a habit of cautious, conditional speech],
  [his training as an economist]
)

#mcq(34, [*The Lekki Headmaster* echoes the title of],
  [a radio programme about education],
  [a Nigerian newspaper column],
  [the television drama *The Village Headmaster*],
  [a novel by Achebe]
)

#mcq(35, [The drama club's dance in Chapter 11 is significant because it],
  [marks the start of the holiday],
  [shows that the pupils dislike Bepo],
  [brings Badagry back on stage and forces his reaction into public],
  [is the only entertainment in the book]
)

#mcq(36, [The novel's treatment of proverbs is best described as],
  [functional — each one carries an argument],
  [decorative],
  [comic],
  [old-fashioned]
)

#mcq(37, [A word or phrase repeated across a novel to build meaning is a],
  [simile],
  [stage direction],
  [sonnet],
  [motif]
)

#mcq(38, [The tone of the novel's treatment of Seri is],
  [mocking],
  [contemptuous],
  [sympathetic but unsentimental],
  [adoring]
)

#mcq(39, [Which statement about the novel's style is accurate?],
  [It uses long periodic sentences and a wide vocabulary.],
  [It is written in verse.],
  [It imitates eighteenth-century prose.],
  [It uses short sentences, ordinary vocabulary and frequent idiom.]
)

#mcq(40, [The novel's use of chapter titles is best described as],
  [structural, because the first and last titles frame the book],
  [accidental],
  [decorative],
  [comic]
)

