  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Third person, confined to Bepo — the pronoun is *he*, and only his knowledge is available.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Other people's private thoughts are outside the narrator's reach — which is why the judgements in the book are Bepo's and can be wrong.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The assembly is in the morning, so the title must be figurative: a man standing in the last of the light.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Dawn reverses dusk: the same man, walking back into the light. The ellipsis makes the day still in the making.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A frame: the two moods enclose the twelve chapters and make the middle a test the ending has already decided.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A proverb — a fixed saying offered as wisdom — used metaphorically: the staff, fed and lent money, may one day strike.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    *Like* makes it a simile; the fact that the sentence is also a proverb does not change the grammatical device.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    One sentence carries the class anxiety, the fear of competition and the decision that follows — which is what proverbs are for.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The shout makes the inner argument public, and it rehearses the same word in the dream on the plane.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A recurring word that gathers meaning each time it appears — the mark of a motif rather than an accident.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The slave ship returns in his sleep, and the decision is made in the space between the dream and the boarding call.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The carvings are his students and the school is the workshop — the proverb is Chapter 11's argument in one line.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It puts the theme in the mouths of the people living it, and it locates the book in its own street.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Code-mixing is the setting heard rather than described; the food in Bepo's luggage is the same technique as the market he bought it in.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    *Accessible* is the word; *simple* is the judgement the paper will not reward, because the plainness is a deliberate choice.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The wag says the thing the chapter cannot say in its own voice — which is why his jokes land at the worst moments.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The argument is inside the man, not on a placard. The irony is never stated, which is why it is effective.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The assembly breaks into the school's victory song and the principal is carried — the book ends on a deliberately hopeful note.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The ritual at Beesway is told where it is needed — in the chapter about the corruption he has already refused.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The length of the service is the argument: this is not a job he drifted into and can leave lightly.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A flashback moves the narrative backwards; foreshadowing points forwards, and an aside speaks to the reader.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Foreshadowing — the shout in Chapter 11 prepares the dream in Chapter 12, and the Badagry visit prepares both.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    *Ade as Well as Jide COMES vs. COME* — the grammar row is announced in the title, which is unusual in the book.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Point of No Return is the site at Badagry; the waterfalls are elsewhere in the country and the proverb belongs to the board.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The place where the enslaved were put on ships becomes the name for every leaving that cannot be reversed.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Neither title describes the hour of its chapter — which is the signal that both are figurative.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A motif: the object recurs and gathers meaning. The slave ship, the snake and the carvings all work this way.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Everything arrives through Bepo's mind, so his arithmetic and his excuses arrive with the weight of fact.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    London is a voice, a set of figures and a dream. Taking the reader there would make the novel a comparison; leaving it out keeps the book a decision.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A sad story with jokes in it is readable; a sermon is not. The humour is a structural choice, not decoration.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    *Laughing Waterfalls* names the excursions. The other three name moods or a persuasion.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The present runs forward while the past arrives in blocks, and the two timelines meet at Badagry.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is a verbal tic that hedges every claim — the speech of a man who argues for a living.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The nickname plays on the old drama, which is why the joke became a title of respect rather than a mockery.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The past is performed in front of him, and he shouts before he can stop himself.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The snake, the carver and the rest are load-bearing: remove them and the scene loses its argument, not just its flavour.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A motif. In this novel the strongest are the shout, the ship and the two chapter titles.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    She is given the best material case in the book and no villainy; the novel neither excuses nor accuses her.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Short sentences, ordinary words and a steady supply of idiom — the plainness that keeps a sad story readable.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    *Dusk* and *…Dawn* enclose the twelve chapters, and a question about either title is a question about structure.
  ]

]

